1 What bltzread is
******************

bltzread helps you read text in a language you are learning.  It colours
every word by how well you know it, shows a small card with the
dictionary entry when you rest on a word, and remembers your progress.
When a word becomes fully known, it can be added to your bltzr skip
list, so bltzr stops listing it as new vocabulary.

   bltzread never changes the text.  It only draws colours on top of it,
and the buffer is read-only while the mode is on.  It works in any
buffer: a plain text file, a web page in EWW, or a book opened with
nov.el.

   This manual says what every feature and option does.  The separate
_bltzread Tutorial_ walks you through using it step by step.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: The five levels,  Next: Starting and stopping,  Prev: What bltzread is,  Up: Top

2 The five levels
*****************

Every word has a level:

Level 1, New
     You have just met it.  Strongest colour.
Level 2, Recognized
     You have seen it and half remember it.
Level 3, Familiar
     You usually understand it.
Level 4, Learned
     You nearly always understand it.  Only an underline.
Level 5, Known
     You know it.  No colour.  This is where a word may go onto your
     skip list.
Ignored
     Names, numbers and other words you do not want to learn.  Shown
     dimly and never counted.

   Words on your bltzr skip lists start out as level 5.  Words bltzread
has never seen start at level 1.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Starting and stopping,  Next: Changing a word's level,  Prev: The five levels,  Up: Top

3 Starting and stopping
***********************

Run ‘M-x bltzread-mode’ in a buffer.  bltzread asks for the language (a
three letter code such as ‘slv’ or ‘pli’) unless the buffer already has
one or you have set a default.  It asks bltzr about the words, then
paints them.

   Running ‘M-x bltzread-mode’ again turns it off and makes the buffer
writable again.

   To read text you have copied from somewhere else, run ‘M-x
bltzread-read-text’.  It uses the selected region, or the clipboard, or
asks you to type the text.  It opens a comfortable reading page with
larger type, wider line spacing and side margins.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Changing a word's level,  Next: The word card,  Prev: Starting and stopping,  Up: Top

4 Changing a word's level
*************************

A level applies to the whole word: setting ‘3’ on _hiše_ also sets
_hiša_ and _hišo_ (see ‘bltzread-level-scope’).  Clean reads, in
contrast, count only for the form you actually read.  Numbers such as
‘24’, ‘5.26’ or ‘2,81’ are never marked.

   Put the cursor on a word and press:

‘1’ to ‘5’
     Set that level.
‘i’
     Ignore the word.  Pressing ‘i’ on an ignored word brings back its
     old level.
‘u’
     Undo the last bltzread change even when other edits came in
     between.  Ordinary ‘undo’ (‘C-/’, ‘C-x u’) and ‘undo-redo’ (‘C-?’)
     also take back and restore bltzread changes (level, ignore, note,
     lookup, finished session) in order with everything else, although
     the buffer is read-only.
‘c’
     Close the card.  It returns when you move to another word.
‘d’ and ‘D’
     Show the next or previous dictionary entry when a word has several.
‘l’
     Look it up in the dictionary (also double-click with the mouse).
‘N’
     Write yourself a note about the word.  The note is shown in the
     card.
‘}’ and ‘{’
     Jump to the next or previous word that is still unknown.  Give a
     count to jump further: ‘C-5 }’ moves five unknown words on (‘M-5’
     or ‘C-u 5’ in a terminal).  A negative count goes the other way.
     If fewer remain, bltzread moves as far as it can and says how far.

   These are single keys with no modifier.  In a subtitle buffer (meow
motion state, where only ‘j’ and ‘k’ are taken) they are free.  The two
packages read alike: ‘{}’ are unknown words, ‘()’ are cues.

   All of these also work after the prefix ‘s’ (‘bltzread-prefix-key’),
for example ‘s 3’.  The prefix also gives:

‘s f’
     Finish the reading session (see below).
‘s k’ or ‘s K’
     Mark every word in the range you pick as known.
‘s r’
     Open the review list.
‘s m’
     Change how you pick the range you read.
‘s <’ and ‘s >’
     Set the start and end of the range yourself.
‘s a’
     Annotate: read the words again and paint them.
‘s L’
     Change the buffer's language.
‘s .’
     Show the card for the word under the cursor.
‘s n’ and ‘s p’
     Next and previous unknown word (like ‘}’ and ‘{’).

   ‘M-x bltzread-avy-unknown’ (in ‘bltzread-avy.el’, needs the ‘avy’
package) tags every unknown word in view with an avy hint.  Type a hint
to go to that word and open its card.  Escape or ‘C-g’ removes the tags
and continues as ‘avy-goto-char-timer’.  It has no key by default; bind
it, or run it from a hook (see _Using bltzread with subtle_).


File: bltzread.info,  Node: The word card,  Next: The dictionary,  Prev: Changing a word's level,  Up: Top

5 The word card
***************

When the cursor rests on a word for a moment, a small card appears.  It
shows the word, its basic form, its level, how many clean reads it has,
your note, and the dictionary entry.  In a graphical Emacs the card
floats above the word, always above and never below: if there is no
room, the window is scrolled to make some.  The card follows the word
when the text scrolls.  It disappears when you switch buffer, window or
frame.  After you set a level the card hides; it returns when you leave
the word and come back, or when you press ‘s .’.  By default
(‘bltzread-popup-only-on-motion’) a card opens only after a command
moved the cursor onto a word, so text scrolled for you, for example by
subtle, neither opens cards nor counts lookups.  In a terminal it
appears in a window at the bottom.

   Looking a word up with ‘l’ shows a longer entry.  Lookups are counted
once per word per session.  Merely passing over a word, for example with
‘}’, is not a lookup: with the default ‘bltzread-lookup-counts’
(‘command’) only ‘l’ counts; set it to ‘interaction’ to let ‘d’, ‘D’ and
‘s .’ count too.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: The dictionary,  Next: Reading sessions how words rise by themselves,  Prev: The word card,  Up: Top

6 The dictionary
****************

bltzread uses sdcv, a command-line dictionary program that reads
StarDict dictionaries.  You tell bltzread where your dictionaries are
and which to use for each language.  If sdcv or the dictionary is
missing, the card simply has no entry; nothing breaks.

   A dictionary can be named by its exact name, its folder name (‘dpd’),
or any part of the name that picks out only one.  A language with no
entry in ‘bltzread-sdcv-dictionaries’ searches no dictionary at all (see
‘bltzread-sdcv-unlisted-languages’), so Slovene entries never turn up in
Pali.  bltzread warns once when a configured name matches nothing.  ‘M-x
bltzread-dict-diagnose’ shows the program, the folder, every dictionary
found and what each language uses, and runs a test lookup.

   Some big dictionaries, such as the Digital Pāḷi Dictionary, have an
index that is not in the order sdcv expects, so exact lookups miss
almost every word.  ‘tools/sdcv-fix-index.py SRC OUT’ writes a fixed
copy and leaves the original alone.  Point
‘bltzread-sdcv-data-dir-alist’ at a folder holding the copy.

   You can replace sdcv with your own function (see the options).


