7.1 Telling bltzread what you read
==================================

There are four ways, chosen by ‘bltzread-range-method’.  Pick the one
that feels best; you can try them in ‘M-x bltzread-range-playground’.

farthest
     bltzread proposes everything from where you started to the farthest
     place you reached.  You confirm or adjust.  By default the place
     reached is the bottom of the text on screen.  With
     ‘bltzread-range-farthest-source’ set to a function (for example
     subtle's ‘subtle-played-through-position’) it is whatever that
     function returns, so only text that was played counts.
     ‘bltzread-range-start-function’ does the same for the start,
     because a played run need not begin at the top.
points
     You mark a start and an end yourself with ‘s <’ and ‘s >’.
paragraphs
     Paragraph numbers appear in the margin; you type from and to.
lines
     The same, using line numbers.

   After the range is counted, bltzread may move your bookmark to the
end of it.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Pages and chapters,  Prev: Telling bltzread what you read,  Up: Reading sessions how words rise by themselves

7.2 Pages and chapters
======================

In nov.el, turning a page replaces the text, so bltzread paints the new
page and forgets the old range.  Finish the session (‘s f’) before you
turn the page if you want that reading counted.  Closing the buffer also
asks.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: The review list,  Next: Your skip lists,  Prev: Reading sessions how words rise by themselves,  Up: Top

8 The review list
*****************

Words that reach level 5 are not put on your skip list straight away.
They wait in a queue.  ‘M-x bltzread-review’ (or ‘s r’) shows the queue:

‘a’
     Add this word (every word starts as add).
‘d’
     Decide later.  It stays in the queue.
‘r’
     Reject.  The word drops back to level 4.
‘A’
     Mark every word add.
‘x’
     Apply: write the added words to the skip list.

   bltzread makes a ‘.bak’ copy of the list file before it writes.  If
you prefer, words can be written at once without review.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Your skip lists,  Next: Where bltzread keeps its data,  Prev: The review list,  Up: Top

9 Your skip lists
*****************

bltzr has two skip lists: exact words (only the spelling you list) and
word families (a basic word and all its forms).  By default bltzread
adds the exact spelling you read to the exact-words list.  You can
choose word families instead, or be asked each time, and set this
separately per language.

   bltzread finds the files in your bltzr settings file
(‘~/.config/bltzr/bltzr.toml’).  You can name the files yourself
instead.


File: bltzread.info,  Node: Where bltzread keeps its data,  Next: Options,  Prev: Your skip lists,  Up: Top

10 Where bltzread keeps its data
********************************

One file per language, in ‘bltzread-state-directory’ (by default a
folder named ‘bltzread’ in your Emacs directory).  It holds levels,
clean-read counts, notes, the queue and a short history.  Files are
plain text and saved a few seconds after a change.


